#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124636 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e593685-f5ca-5501-94bf-7cb4d49e935b" :knowledge-point/title "The Domain of a Function Involving Reciprocals and Radicals"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124637 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e4fd256-a726-523c-84dc-f62d4e2711cc" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Domain of a Multivariable Function"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124638 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9289c4d-4f7f-5704-a2cb-272f92d8fef3" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Two-Variable Function"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124640 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88f2bc84-03e8-5921-81e2-683a09195279" :knowledge-point/title "The Domain of a Function Containing Exponential, Logarithmic, and Trigonometric Functions"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125865 :tutorial/content "Another way of visualizing a two-variable function is to draw its graph as a **surface** in a three-dimensional coordinate system. \n\nFor example, consider the function\n\n$$\nz = f(x,y) = x^2 +y^2, \\quad (x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the (restricted) domain is given by the unit disc \n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\: : \\: x^2+y^2 \\leq 1 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe graph of $f$ is a surface that consists of all the points with coordinates\n\n$$\n(x, \\, y, \\, f(x,y)), \\quad (x,y) \\in D,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/a3/a3c9c7eca49a0f828e53c0775afac16335033c1a8881367e49098bf7125ee2e6.png)\n\n  \n\nNotice that range of $f$ is represented by the interval $R=[0,1]$ on the vertical $z$-axis. It consists of the possible $z$-coordinates of the points on our surface." :tutorial/id #uuid "9bf49e76-e25e-5191-ad7f-95028f749c37" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6920 :tutorial/title "The Graph of a Two-Variable Function"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125868 :tutorial/content "A function $f(x,y)$ of two variables is a rule that maps each pair of numbers $(x,y)\\in D$ to a unique real number. Under this notation, $D\\subseteq \\mathbb R^2$ is the **domain** of $f.$\n\nFor instance, consider the following two-variable function: \n\n$$\nf(x,y) = \\dfrac{\\sin(xy)}{\\sqrt{x-1}}\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this function by substituting concrete pairs of numbers for $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n- Let's evaluate $f(x,y)$ at the point $(2, \\pi).$ To do that, we substitute $x=2$ and $y=\\pi$ into the function expression: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,\\pi ) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)}{\\sqrt{x-1}}{|}_{(2,\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\pi )}{\\sqrt{2-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(2,\\pi)$ returned a unique real number, this means that $(2,\\pi)$ belongs to the domain of $f,$ i.e., $(2,\\pi)\\in D.$\n\n- Let's now try to evaluate $f(x,y)$ at the point $(0, \\pi).$ To do that, we substitute $x=0$ and $y=\\pi$ into the function expression: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,\\pi ) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)}{\\sqrt{x-1}}{|}_{(0,\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0\\cdot \\pi )}{\\sqrt{0-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{-1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined over the real numbers.\n\nSince $f(0,\\pi)$ did not return a unique *real* number, this means that $(0,\\pi)\\notin D.$\n\nWe can also have functions of three variables $f(x,y,z),$ functions of four variables, $f(x_1,x_2,x_3,x_4),$ etc. There is no limit to the number of variables a function can have. Functions with more than one variable are called **multivariable functions.**" :tutorial/id #uuid "8d127fec-12ff-5644-b095-33e9b94a52d1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7034 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125869 :tutorial/content "The **domain** of a multivariable function is the set of points where the function is defined. \n\nFor example, consider the function \n\n$$\nz = f(x,y) =  \\sqrt{1 -x^2 -y^2}.\n$$\n\nThe function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the expression under the square root is non-negative. As a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n1 - x^2 - y^2 \\geq 0 \n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nx^2 + y^2 \\leq 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\,:\\, x^2 + y^2 \\leq 1 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nGeometrically, the domain of our function is a disc of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane, as shown on the left diagram below.\n\n![](images/86/8642db9fd933ec2f15c6712c713cfaec8fb20052066dcca3915904190d3551fa.png)\n\nEach point in this domain is uniquely mapped to a real number $z.$ Possible values of $z$ can be visualized on a separate axis (depicted by the diagram on the right). \n\nIf $(x,y)$ is allowed to run over the entire domain, the set of points on the $z$-axis where the values of $f(x,y)$ land is called the **range** of the function. For this function, the range is $z\\in [0,1].$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- When discussing a function's domain, we usually refer to the so-called **natural domain**, the largest set of values for which the function is defined.\n\n- We can also *restrict* the domain of a function to a subset of the natural domain. For example, we might restrict the domain of $f$ to all values of $y$ in the upper-half plane. In this case, we'd write\n\n$$\nf(x,y) =  \\sqrt{1 -x^2 -y^2}, \\quad (x,y)\\in D, \\quad D = \\big\\{ (x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\,:\\, x^2 + y^2 \\leq 1, \\: y\\geq 0 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThis gives a domain/range diagram that looks as follows.\n\n![](images/fb/fbab832d984232b0b3b951579e5ee2727e92c9f4bf496d4fffe82f7230cfbb14.png)\n\nIn this case, restricting the domain does not affect the range. However, in general, restricting the domain *will* affect the range." :tutorial/id #uuid "d81a3bfc-ca0f-528c-ad48-e606581c6d6c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7035 :tutorial/title "The Domain and Range of a Two-Variable Function"}]}
